#!/usr/bin/env python3
"exec" "python3" "$0" "$@"  # a shell that is handed this file re-execs python; python sees a bare string
__doc__ = """decisions — index, size-check and archive the per-project decisions.md files.

WHY THIS EXISTS. Every project CLAUDE.md said "read this before proposing
anything" about a file that grows append-only. Measured 2026-08-24:

    lane-a/decisions.md             2097 lines / 120K   72 entries
    setup/decisions.md              1416 lines / 144K   41 entries, all since 07-25
    lane-b/decisions.md              716 lines /  48K   11 entries
    lane-c/decisions.md              253 lines /  16K   14 entries

setup gained 41 entries in 30 days. Extrapolated that is ~500/year and ~1.7MB —
a file no agent can be told to read, so the instruction would quietly become one
that gets ignored, which is worse than a large file. Nothing loads decisions.md
automatically (no @-import, no hook); the cost is paid by whoever is told to
read it, once per session, per executor.

So: the instruction becomes SEEK, not READ (`decisions index`), and the file
gets rotated before it outgrows a seek (`decisions archive`).

    decisions check                     # sizes and what is over the threshold
    decisions index [project] [-n N]     # the last N decisions, newest first
    decisions archive <project> [--keep N | --before YYYY-MM-DD] [--apply]
    decisions supersede <project> [--apply]   # retire the rulings a later one reverses

AN ENTRY IS A RULING: four fields, one sentence each, and the narrative in
engram rather than in the file. Measured 2026-09-08, this is where the size
actually comes from — three lanes ran 38-53 physical lines
per entry while another, which had already settled on a fixed set of fields, ran
17.8 for MORE entries than any of them. The file was never big because the
project takes many decisions.

    ## YYYY-MM-DD — <title>
    **Ruling:** one sentence.
    **Rules out:** what is now off the table.
    **Reverses:** #<the engram id of the ruling this replaces> | none
    **Evidence:** engram #NNNNN

AND `Reverses` IS WHAT MAKES THE FILE STOP GROWING. Until it existed, a decision
that had been overturned stayed in the live file next to the one that overturned
it, because nothing could read the reversal out of prose. `supersede` moves the
loser to the archive, `index` marks it, and `check` names the ones still sitting
live. Growth is no longer monotonic in the number of decisions taken.

A RULING IS NAMED BY ITS ENGRAM ID, WHICH IS WHY `Evidence` IS MANDATORY. That
field is not provenance any more; it is the identity `Reverses` points at. The
identity was the title until 2026-09-08, and a title cannot carry it: titles are
prose, they get edited, they are not unique, and an em dash and a hyphen read as
two different titles. An entry with `Evidence: none` — every entry predating
this format — is readable and standing, but it cannot be reversed, only
rewritten. `decisions check` names those.

Archiving and superseding are both dry runs unless --apply. Entries move to
`<project>/decisions/<YYYY>-Q<n>.md`; the live file keeps its preamble, its
newest KEEP_DEFAULT entries, and a pointer to the archive. Rotation is by count,
not by calendar: one lane put 67 entries into one quarter, so a quarterly cutoff
would have moved nothing while the file was already twice the threshold. The
archive files are still named by quarter, because a name that stops changing is
what makes an old link keep working. Nothing is deleted: an entry is
either in the live file or in exactly one archive file, and `decisions index`
reads both.

TWO RULES THIS TOOL NOW ENFORCES, because it broke both of them in the field.

ORDER IS MEASURED, NEVER ASSUMED. These files are append-only, so the newest
entry is at the BOTTOM. `archive` selected the leading N entries in file order
and therefore kept the OLDEST and archived the NEWEST — measured on the real
setup file 2026-09-08 (live kept 09-01/03/06, the archive took 09-07/08) and
reverted in 2d831ff. Its count check, 136 entries in and 136 out, was blind to
it: a verdict about an ordered file has to look at the order. Selection now runs
over an explicitly chronological list, and a `## YYYY-MM-DD HH:MM` clock in the
heading orders same-day entries instead of leaving them in append order.

WHAT IT DOES NOT RECOGNISE, IT REFUSES — it does not drop it. A `## ` heading
without a date in it was not an entry to the rotator, so fourteen of them were
rewritten out of BOTH files while the command printed
`25 entries stay, 18 move / wrote / rewrote` and exited 0 (recovered from git,
formats fixed in efae689). The sentinel skip made it worse: anything below a
rotation pointer was read as this tool's own bookkeeping, so an undated entry
there was invisible to `index` — the only path any CLAUDE.md points at — and
deleted by the next rotation. An unrecognised heading is now a third value with a
name: `check` fails on it and `index` says which headings it cannot list, so it
stops being silently absent. Rotation CARRIES it — as body text of the entry
above it, into whichever file that entry lands in — and proves it arrived, which
is the next paragraph. Refusing to rotate on one was tried first and reverted the
same day: 77 of them sit in the four projects' archive files and every project's
merge target is the same quarter, so the refusal blocked all four while
`hw status` went on telling brainers to rotate. And a `## ` inside a fenced code
block is example text, not an entry; the old parser wrote a `2099-Q1.md` archive
file out of one.

AND IT RE-READS WHAT IT WROTE. The counts used to be announced from an in-memory
budget estimate taken BEFORE the write, and the estimate's escape hatch was a
hardcoded `len(entries) // 5` — a fraction, not a measurement. One oversized
entry got `nothing to archive`, exit 0, on a file `check` called OVER in the same
breath. Retention is now chosen by RENDERING the prospective file and counting
its actual lines. Every output is then STAGED, and the whole entry multiset —
heading and body across the live file and every archive, not a total — is
compared against the inputs before a single file is installed; a rotation that
would lose or duplicate anything writes nothing at all. The installed files are
re-read afterwards, and that re-read is the only thing this command prints as a
result.
"""

import datetime
import json
import os
import re
import sys
import tempfile

# NATIVE WINDOWS (Git Bash): bin/msys-compat.sh puts bin/ on PYTHONPATH for the
# entry points it starts; run by hand, this file loads bin/sitecustomize.py
# itself (a no-op when Python already did). Elsewhere nothing happens.
if os.name == "nt":
    sys.path.insert(0, os.path.dirname(os.path.realpath(__file__)))
    import sitecustomize  # noqa: E402,F401

ROOT = os.path.dirname(os.path.dirname(os.path.realpath(__file__)))


def _lanes():
    """-> (projects, aliases): every lane with a decisions.md, and its spellings.

    Read from the lane table, projects.json at the brain root, so a new lane
    reaches this tool by having a decisions.md — nothing here names one. The
    order is the table's `lanes` order, the one hw names lanes in. The aliases
    are each lane's hw_aliases and brain_aliases, plus `brain` for the lane
    whose checkout IS the brain root.

    A tree with no readable table (a test fixture holding only bin/ and one
    lane directory) takes every directory beside bin/ that has a decisions.md,
    sorted, and no aliases: the directory is the only fact available there.
    """
    try:
        with open(os.path.join(ROOT, "projects.json"), encoding="utf-8") as fh:
            table = json.load(fh).get("lanes")
    except (OSError, ValueError, AttributeError):
        table = None
    aliases = {}
    if isinstance(table, dict):
        names = list(table)
        for lane, spec in table.items():
            spec = spec if isinstance(spec, dict) else {}
            for alias in (spec.get("hw_aliases") or []) + (spec.get("brain_aliases") or []):
                aliases.setdefault(alias, lane)
            if spec.get("checkout") == "{brain}":
                aliases.setdefault("brain", lane)
    else:
        try:
            # A lane's old name left behind as a symlink to its new directory
            # is not a second lane.
            names = sorted(n for n in os.listdir(ROOT)
                           if not os.path.islink(os.path.join(ROOT, n)))
        except OSError:
            names = []
    projects = tuple(n for n in names if os.path.isfile(os.path.join(ROOT, n, "decisions.md")))
    return projects, aliases


PROJECTS, ALIASES = _lanes()

# The threshold is a seek budget, not an aesthetic. `decisions index` on a file
# this size is ~90 headings, which is still one screen of scrolling and one cheap
# read for an agent. Past it, the index itself starts costing what the file used
# to.
WARN_LINES = 1200

# How many entries stay in the live file when `archive` runs with no cutoff.
# Sized so the live file stays under WARN_LINES at this repo's observed ~30
# lines per entry, with room for the next few weeks of appends.
KEEP_DEFAULT = 25

# A heading is an entry when the DATE is in the heading, and the optional clock
# after it is what orders two entries written on the same day. Without it,
# `index` fell back to append order for same-day rows, so the tool had two
# separate broken notions of order (see the docstring). The clock is optional
# because most existing entries do not carry one; it is never invented.
ENTRY_RE = re.compile(
    r"^## (\d{4})-(\d{2})-(\d{2})"
    r"(?:[ T](\d{2}):(\d{2})(?::(\d{2}))?)?"
    r"\b(.*)$"
)

# Any level-2 heading at all. The difference between this and ENTRY_RE is the
# whole "third value" rule: a `## ` that is not an entry and not this tool's own
# pointer heading is neither, and neither means REFUSE, not drop.
H2_RE = re.compile(r"^## ")

# A fenced block's contents are literal text, including a line that looks like a
# dated heading. The old parser had no fence state, so a `## 2099-03-01` inside
# a ```markdown example was an entry, and rotation wrote a 2099-Q1.md archive
# file out of it.
FENCE_RE = re.compile(r"^ {0,3}(`{3,}|~{3,})")

# The pointer `archive` appends to the live file has no date, so `split` would
# read it as the body of the OLDEST KEPT entry and the next rotation would carry
# it into the archive. Measured on a copy, 2026-08-24: it did exactly that. The
# sentinel marks that block so it can be SKIPPED while parsing, and rotation
# stays idempotent.
#
# It used to CUT the text at the sentinel instead, which is the same thing only
# while the pointer is the last thing in the file. It is not, for long: these
# files are append-only and a new entry goes at the bottom, below a pointer a
# previous rotation left behind. Measured on the live files 2026-09-01 — one lane
# hid 32 of its 58 entries, another 9 of 40, the whole pilot — and
# `index` is the ONLY way any CLAUDE.md tells an agent to reach these files, so
# the newest decisions were unreachable by the supported path. Rotation would
# then have deleted them, because it rebuilds the live file from what `split`
# returned. Skip the block; never cut the file.
#
# And the block ENDS at the next `## ` heading of any kind, not only at a dated
# one. Ending it only on a date is what let an undated entry below a pointer be
# swallowed as bookkeeping: measured 2026-09-08, `index` could not see it and
# the next rotation deleted it while reporting success.
POINTER_MARK = "<!-- decisions:archive-pointer -->"
POINTER_HEADING = "## Older quarters are archived"
# The two per-project lines of the block `pointer_block()` writes, anchored end
# to end. Anchored on purpose: see `carried_from_region`.
GENERATED_POINTER_LINE = re.compile(
    r"^(?:"
    r"Closed quarters live in `[^`/]+/decisions/`\. They are not superseded — a"
    r"|`Rules out` line in there still rules it out\. `decisions index [^`]+ --all`"
    r")$"
)


def die(msg):
    print("decisions: %s" % msg, file=sys.stderr)
    raise SystemExit(1)


def resolve(name):
    name = ALIASES.get(name, name)
    if name not in PROJECTS:
        die("unknown project %r — one of: %s" % (name, ", ".join(PROJECTS)))
    return name


def project_of(path):
    """-> the project a decisions file belongs to, or None.

    Needed because the pointer block is per-project, and only THIS project's
    block may be subtracted from THIS file's pointer region. A verbatim quote of
    another project's block is somebody's text.
    """
    real = os.path.realpath(path)
    for project in PROJECTS:
        if real.startswith(os.path.realpath(os.path.join(ROOT, project)) + os.sep):
            return project
    return None


def live_path(project):
    return os.path.join(ROOT, project, "decisions.md")


def archive_dir(project):
    return os.path.join(ROOT, project, "decisions")


def stray_temps(project):
    """-> staged temp files left in a project's archive directory.

    THE REASON THESE WERE INVISIBLE is one line up: `archive_files` filters on
    `.md`, so a `.decisions-*.tmp` is not merely unreported, it is unreachable
    by every command that walks a project's files.

    The rotation now cleans up after its own failures, and this is still not
    redundant: a process that is KILLED — SIGKILL, a full disk mid-write, the
    machine going down — never runs its cleanup at all. Nothing the writer does
    can cover that case, so something has to be able to NAME what it left.
    """
    d = archive_dir(project)
    if not os.path.isdir(d):
        return []
    return sorted(
        os.path.join(d, f) for f in os.listdir(d)
        if f.startswith(".decisions-") and f.endswith(".tmp")
    )


def archive_files(project):
    d = archive_dir(project)
    if not os.path.isdir(d):
        return []
    return sorted(
        (os.path.join(d, f) for f in os.listdir(d) if f.endswith(".md")),
        reverse=True,
    )


def entry_key(m):
    """-> (Y, M, D, h, m, s), or None when the heading's date is not a real date.

    An untimed entry sorts at 00:00:00 of its day and its ties are broken by
    position, not by a clock this tool made up.
    """
    y, mo, d = int(m.group(1)), int(m.group(2)), int(m.group(3))
    hh = int(m.group(4)) if m.group(4) else 0 # MUTATION-ANCHOR: 100-M03
    mm = int(m.group(5)) if m.group(5) else 0 # MUTATION-ANCHOR: 100-M03b
    ss = int(m.group(6)) if m.group(6) else 0 # MUTATION-ANCHOR: 100-M03c
    try:
        datetime.datetime(y, mo, d, hh, mm, ss)
    except ValueError:
        return None
    return (y, mo, d, hh, mm, ss)


# ── the ruling format ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# An entry used to be prose with whatever emphasis its author felt like. That is
# why these files grew: measured 2026-09-08, three lanes run
# 38-53 physical lines per entry while another — which had already settled on a
# fixed set of bolded fields — runs 17.8 for MORE entries than any of them. The
# file is not big because the project takes many decisions; it is big because
# each one was written at forty lines instead of five.
#
# So an entry is now a RULING: four named fields, one sentence each.
#
#     ## YYYY-MM-DD — <title>
#     **Ruling:** one sentence.
#     **Rules out:** what is now off the table.
#     **Reverses:** #<the engram id of the ruling this replaces> | none
#     **Evidence:** engram #NNNNN
#
# The narrative — the measurement, the argument, the two attempts that failed —
# goes to engram, which is where a full account belongs and where nobody pays
# for it on every seek. `Evidence` is the pointer back to it, which is why it is
# a field and not a footnote.
#
# THE FIELDS ARE READ ONE LINE AT A TIME, DELIBERATELY. setup/hooks/decisions-check.py
# lints them with a single-line `fullmatch`, and a parser that accepted a
# wrapped value would disagree with the linter about what a file says — the
# reader and the gate would then be enforcing two different formats, which is
# worse than either format. A value that does not fit on one line is a value
# that belongs in engram.
RULING_KEYS = ("Ruling", "Rules out", "Reverses", "Evidence")

# `**Key:** value`, anchored on both ends so a bolded phrase MID-SENTENCE is not
# read as a field. The linter builds the same shape per key; this is the general
# form, and `ruling_fields` filters it down to RULING_KEYS so the two agree.
FIELD_RE = re.compile(r"^\*\*([^:*]+):\*\*\s*(.+)$")

# `Reverses:` NAMES A RULING BY ITS ENGRAM ID, and `none` is the ordinary case.
#
# It named the target by TITLE until 2026-09-08, and that was wrong by
# measurement rather than by taste: a title is prose. It gets edited for clarity
# long after the ruling is settled, it is not unique across a project's history,
# and the dash inside it is not one character — a title written with an em dash
# did not resolve against the same title typed with a hyphen, so the reversal
# pointed at nothing and said nothing about it. An engram id is stable, unique
# and immutable, and it is the one identity a decision already has.
#
# THE CONSEQUENCE, STATED BECAUSE IT CHANGES WHAT `Evidence` IS FOR. `Evidence`
# used to be provenance: a pointer a curious reader could follow. It is now the
# IDENTITY by which another ruling reverses this one, so it is load-bearing. An
# entry with no engram observation behind it cannot be reversed at all — see
# `entry_id`, which is where that gap is handled rather than hidden.
NO_REVERSE = "none"

# `#NNNNN`, `NNNNN` and `engram #NNNNN` are one id. Accepting all three is not
# laxity: `Evidence:` is written `engram #NNNNN` while `Reverses:` is written
# `#NNNNN`, so a tool that read those as different identities would never match
# a single reversal to its target.
# `[0-9]`, NOT `\d`. `\d` matches Unicode digits, so a fullwidth id parsed
# successfully, resolved to nothing, and escaped `unreadable_reverses` precisely
# BECAUSE it parsed — a silent no-op that no message named. Found by review on
# 2026-09-08.
ID_RE = re.compile(r"^(?:engram[ \t]+)?#?([0-9]+)$", re.IGNORECASE)


def as_id(value):
    """-> the digits of an engram id, or None when `value` is not one.

    Normalised to the digits alone so the spellings above are one key. Leading
    zeros are kept: engram issues the id, and deciding that two spellings of a
    number are the same number is engram's call, not this tool's.
    """
    if value is None:
        return None
    m = ID_RE.match(value.strip())
    return m.group(1) if m else None


def entry_title(heading):
    """-> an entry heading's title, or None when the heading carries none.

    A LABEL FOR READERS, NOT AN IDENTITY. Every message this tool prints names
    an entry by its title, because that is what a person recognises — but
    nothing RESOLVES by it any more, for the reasons on `NO_REVERSE` above. The
    leading dash is stripped because `## DATE — Title` is the house style and
    the dash is punctuation, not part of the name.
    """
    m = ENTRY_RE.match(heading)
    if not m:
        return None
    t = m.groups()[-1].strip().lstrip("\u2014\u2013- ").strip()
    return t or None


def ruling_fields(body):
    """-> {key: value} for the RULING_KEYS present in an entry body.

    Fence-aware, because a fenced example of the ruling format is example text:
    the same reason `split` tracks fences. Without it, this file's own docstring
    block above would be parsed as a ruling by any tool that read this file.

    A repeated key keeps the FIRST occurrence. Not the last: an entry that
    quotes a previous ruling below its own would otherwise be read as being
    that previous ruling, and the quote is commentary.
    """
    fields = {}
    fence = None
    for line in body:
        fm = FENCE_RE.match(line)
        if fence is not None:
            if fm and fm.group(1)[0] == fence[0] and len(fm.group(1)) >= len(fence):
                fence = None
            continue
        if fm:
            fence = fm.group(1)
            continue
        m = FIELD_RE.match(line)
        if m:
            key = m.group(1).strip()
            if key in RULING_KEYS and key not in fields:
                fields[key] = m.group(2).strip()
    return fields


def entry_id(body):
    """-> the engram id this entry is identified BY, read from its `Evidence`.

    None when the entry cites no observation. THAT IS A REAL STATE, NOT AN
    ERROR: every entry written before the ruling format existed cites nothing a
    tool can resolve, and `Evidence: none` says so honestly. Such an entry
    cannot be the TARGET of a `Reverses` — it has no name to be called by — and
    `check` reports which ones those are rather than minting an id for them. A
    minted id would point at no observation, and a pointer to nothing is worse
    than an admitted gap: the gap is closed by saving the observation, whereas
    the invented id cannot be detected as wrong by anything at all.
    """
    return as_id(ruling_fields(body).get("Evidence"))


def reverses_target(body):
    """-> the engram id this entry reverses, or None when it reverses nothing.

    `none`, an absent field and a value that is not an id all answer None, and
    the first two are the same answer on purpose: a ruling that reverses nothing
    is the ordinary case, and distinguishing "declared none" from "said nothing"
    would put a difference into the data that means nothing to a reader.

    A MALFORMED value answering None is the one that deserves justifying, since
    it is the difference between doing nothing and doing something wrong. This
    function decides which ENTRIES MOVE between files. A value it cannot resolve
    is a value it must not act on, so it declines — and `supersede` reports the
    unresolvable ones separately (`unreadable_reverses`) so declining stays
    visible instead of looking like a file with no reversals in it.
    """
    value = ruling_fields(body).get("Reverses", NO_REVERSE).strip()
    if value.lower() == NO_REVERSE:
        return None
    return as_id(value)


def unreadable_reverses(entries):
    """-> complaints for entries whose `Reverses` is neither `none` nor an id.

    Split out from `reverses_target` because silence and refusal look identical
    from the outside, and only one of them should. See its docstring.
    """
    out = []
    for e in entries:
        value = ruling_fields(e[2]).get("Reverses")
        if value is None or value.strip().lower() == NO_REVERSE:
            continue
        if as_id(value) is None:
            out.append("  %s: Reverses %r is not an engram id (#NNNNN) or %s"
                       % (e[1][3:][:44], value[:32], NO_REVERSE))
    return out


def order_problems(entries, archive, label):
    """-> complaints when `entries`, in the file's own convention, is not chronological.

    THIS IS THE ASSERTION A COUNT CANNOT MAKE, and it is here because a count
    was trusted instead. On 2026-09-08 a rotation moved the NEWEST entries to
    the archive and left the oldest live; every count matched — 136 in, 136 out,
    zero duplicates — and the file was wrecked. The multiset is summed across
    the live file and every archive, so moving entries BETWEEN them cannot
    change it. Order is a separate property and it needs a separate guard.

    Ties pass. Two entries sharing a key are genuinely unordered by their own
    headings, so an assertion about their relative position would be an
    assertion about append order, which is not a fact this tool owns.
    """
    seq = list(reversed(entries)) if archive else list(entries)
    out = []
    for i in range(len(seq) - 1):
        if seq[i][0] > seq[i + 1][0]:
            out.append(
                "  %s is not in chronological order: %s comes before %s"
                % (label, seq[i][1][3:][:48], seq[i + 1][1][3:][:48])
            )
    return out


def split(path, strict=False, with_pointer_anchor=False):
    """-> (preamble, entries, unknown headings, carried text[, later entries]).

    The preamble is everything before the first entry heading, kept verbatim: it
    carries the file's own contract, and rewriting
    it is not this tool's business — including an ARCHIVE file's preamble, which
    the old writer regenerated over the top of whatever a human had put there.

    The third return value is the headings this tool does not recognise. It is a
    value, not an exception, so `check` can report them and `index` can warn;
    `strict=True` is for `archive`, which must refuse before it writes.

    THE FOURTH IS THE POINTER REGION'S CARRIED TEXT, and it exists because this
    function used to DROP it. Everything below the `POINTER_MARK` sentinel was
    skipped, so a note a person or a script appended at the bottom of the live
    file — which is exactly where things get appended — was discarded here and
    lost by the next rotation, under a printed `VERIFIED by re-reading` that
    only ever counted ENTRIES. Measured 2026-09-08:

        NOTE-A-HUMAN-APPENDED-HERE  ->  gone from every file, exit 0

    The region is skipped for one real reason: the block this tool writes is
    regenerated on every rotation, so echoing it back would duplicate it. That
    is a reason to drop THAT BLOCK, not the region. So the generated block is
    subtracted by matching it, and whatever is left is carried.

    The subtraction is deliberately conservative. If the block's wording ever
    drifts, an old line stops matching and gets CARRIED — which duplicates it
    next to the new block, and duplication is visible, reported by the staged
    conservation check, and recoverable. The other way round loses text
    silently. When the two errors are not symmetric, lean on the loud one.

    When `with_pointer_anchor` is true, the fifth value is every dated entry
    after the pointer, in file order. Rotation uses the first one that remains
    live, preserving carried text relative to entries appended below a previous
    pointer even when earlier such entries are archived. The default four-value
    contract is retained for consumers of this parser.
    """
    with open(path, encoding="utf-8") as fh:
        lines = fh.read().splitlines()
    preamble, entries, unknown = [], [], []
    region = []          # the pointer region's lines, verbatim
    pointer_followers = []
    following_pointer = False
    entry_now = None
    in_pointer = False
    fence = None
    for n, line in enumerate(lines, 1):
        fm = FENCE_RE.match(line)
        if fence is not None:
            # Inside a fence nothing is a heading. Only the matching fence
            # character closes it, so a ``` inside a ~~~ block stays literal.
            if fm and fm.group(1)[0] == fence[0] and len(fm.group(1)) >= len(fence):
                fence = None
        elif fm:
            fence = fm.group(1)
        if fence is None and H2_RE.match(line): # MUTATION-ANCHOR: 100-M11
            m = ENTRY_RE.match(line)
            key = entry_key(m) if m else None
            if key is not None:
                in_pointer = False
                if entry_now:
                    entries.append(entry_now)
                entry_now = (key, line, [])
                if following_pointer:
                    pointer_followers.append(entry_now)
                continue
            if in_pointer and line.strip() == POINTER_HEADING: # MUTATION-ANCHOR: 100-M04
                # The one heading this tool writes itself. Exact match, not a
                # substring: a quoted mention of it must not switch the parser
                # into skipping mode. Recorded rather than dropped on sight —
                # the subtraction below is what removes it.
                region.append(line)
                continue
            # Third value. Not an entry, not our bookkeeping — named, never
            # dropped. A heading whose date is not a real date lands here too,
            # rather than being parsed into a month 13.
            in_pointer = False
            unknown.append((n, line))
            if entry_now is None:
                preamble.append(line)
            else:
                entry_now[2].append(line)
            continue
        if fence is None and POINTER_MARK in line:
            in_pointer = True
            following_pointer = True
            continue
        if in_pointer:
            region.append(line)
            continue
        if entry_now is None:
            preamble.append(line)
        else:
            entry_now[2].append(line)
    if entry_now:
        entries.append(entry_now)
    if strict and fence is not None:
        # The one thing that is genuinely ambiguous rather than merely
        # unrecognised: if the fence never closes, this tool cannot tell example
        # text from entries, and guessing is how the 2099-Q1 archive file got
        # written.
        die("%s: unterminated `%s` fence — refusing to guess where it ends"
            % (os.path.relpath(path, ROOT), fence))
    result = (preamble, entries, unknown,
              carried_from_region(region, project_of(path)))
    return result + (pointer_followers,) if with_pointer_anchor else result


def carried_from_region(region, project=None):
    """-> the pointer region's lines with the block THIS TOOL generates removed.

    Blank lines and the generated body are dropped; everything else is kept
    verbatim, including blanks INSIDE what is kept, so a multi-paragraph note
    survives as it was written. Trailing blanks go, because `render` normalises
    the end of the file anyway.
    """
    # Every non-blank line the generator can emit, from any project: the body is
    # per-project only in the project name, so match on the invariant part.
    generated = {POINTER_HEADING}
    if project is not None:
        # ONLY THIS PROJECT'S BLOCK. A verbatim quote of another project's block
        # is a person's text, and subtracting it lost it — measured 2026-09-08
        # on "Closed quarters live in `<lane>/decisions/`. ..." sitting in
        # setup's file. With no project known, nothing per-project is
        # subtracted: carrying costs a duplicate, dropping costs the line.
        for line in pointer_block(project):
            if line.strip():
                generated.add(line)
    def is_generated(line):
        # BLANKS ARE NOT "GENERATED". Treating them as such dropped every blank
        # in the region, which silently reflowed a multi-paragraph note into one
        # block — a smaller loss than the one being fixed, but the same kind.
        # They are kept here and trimmed at the ends below.
        t = line.strip()
        if not t:
            return False
        if t in generated or t == POINTER_MARK:
            return True
        # THE WHOLE GENERATED LINE, NOT A PREFIX OF IT. A `startswith` here ate
        # a human sentence that merely began the same way — measured
        # 2026-09-08 on "Closed quarters live in `somewhere-else/` and this is
        # a person talking." The staged check then refused, so the file became
        # unrotatable instead of losing the line; both are wrong, and the cause
        # was a match looser than the thing it was matching.
        #
        # These two lines are per-project only in the project name, so the
        # pattern pins everything else and lets the name vary. A sentence that
        # differs anywhere else is somebody's, and it is carried.
        return False
    # INSIDE A FENCE NOTHING IS OURS. Measured 2026-09-08, on a fenced example
    # that quoted this tool's own pointer block: the three inner lines were
    # classified as generated and dropped, leaving an empty ``` pair. The
    # staged conservation check REFUSED before writing — which is the guard
    # working, and why nothing was lost — but the result was a legitimate file
    # that could no longer be rotated at all. That is the same shape as the
    # heading refusal that blocked all four projects and was reverted on this
    # same day: a refusal over ordinary content is not a safe default either.
    #
    # `split` already tracks fences for exactly this reason. So does this.
    kept = []
    fence = None
    for ln in region:
        fm = FENCE_RE.match(ln)
        if fence is not None:
            kept.append(ln)
            if fm and fm.group(1)[0] == fence[0] and len(fm.group(1)) >= len(fence):
                fence = None
            continue
        if fm:
            fence = fm.group(1)
            kept.append(ln)
            continue
        if not is_generated(ln):
            kept.append(ln)
    # Keep interior blanks, drop the ones at either end.
    keep_from, keep_to = 0, len(kept)
    while keep_from < keep_to and not kept[keep_from].strip():
        keep_from += 1
    while keep_to > keep_from and not kept[keep_to - 1].strip():
        keep_to -= 1
    return kept[keep_from:keep_to]


def is_archive(path):
    return os.path.dirname(os.path.realpath(path)).endswith(os.sep + "decisions")


def in_order(path, entries):
    """-> the file's entries OLDEST FIRST, by the file's own convention.

    The live file is append-only, so its file order IS chronological; archive
    files are written newest-first, so theirs is reversed. The sort is stable,
    so entries sharing a key keep that chronology instead of being reshuffled.
    This function exists because the bug it replaces was selecting `entries[:n]`
    and calling the result "the newest".
    """
    seq = list(reversed(entries)) if is_archive(path) else list(entries) # MUTATION-ANCHOR: 100-M02
    return sorted(seq, key=lambda e: e[0])


def quarter(date):
    return "%d-Q%d" % (date[0], (date[1] - 1) // 3 + 1)


def line_count(text):
    return text.count("\n") + (0 if text.endswith("\n") else 1)


def canon(body):
    """An entry's body with its trailing blank separator lines removed.

    The writer puts one blank line between entries, so a body read back from a
    written file has one the input did not. Comparing bodies is the point of the
    conservation check — a total was not enough, twice — so the separator has to
    be normalised out and nothing else.
    """
    out = list(body)
    while out and not out[-1].strip():
        out.pop()
    return tuple(out)


def multiset(paths):
    """-> {(key, heading, body): count} across the given files.

    An entry belongs in exactly one place across a project, so this is counted
    over the live file AND every archive together, and a count of 2 is as much a
    failure as a count of 0.
    """
    bag = {}
    for path in paths:
        if not os.path.exists(path):
            continue
        _pre, entries, _unknown, _carried = split(path)
        for key, heading, body in entries:
            k = (key, heading, canon(body))
            bag[k] = bag.get(k, 0) + 1
    return bag


def render(preamble, entries, trailer=(), trailer_before=None):
    out = list(preamble)
    trailer_written = False
    for entry in entries:
        if entry is trailer_before:
            out.extend(trailer)
            if trailer and out and out[-1].strip():
                out.append("")
            trailer_written = True
        _key, heading, body = entry
        out.append(heading)
        out.extend(body)
        if out[-1].strip():
            out.append("")
    if not trailer_written:
        out.extend(trailer)
    return "\n".join(out).rstrip() + "\n"


def pointer_block(project):
    return [
        "",
        POINTER_MARK,
        "",
        POINTER_HEADING,
        "",
        "Closed quarters live in `%s/decisions/`. They are not superseded — a" % project,
        "`Rules out` line in there still rules it out. `decisions index %s --all`" % project,
        "lists every entry across both, and `rg` reads them together.",
        "",
    ]


def archive_preamble(project, q):
    return [
        "# %s — decisions, %s (archive)" % (project, q),
        "",
        "Closed quarter, newest first. Same contract as `../decisions.md`: a",
        "decision and what it rules out are the same entry. Nothing here was",
        "reversed by being archived — find it with `decisions index %s --all`," % project,
        "or `rg` this directory.",
        "",
        "---",
        "",
    ]


def stage(path, text):
    """Write `text` beside `path`, fsync it, read the bytes back, return the temp path.

    Nothing is installed here. The read-back is the point: a staged file whose
    bytes are not what we rendered never reaches its destination.
    """
    d = os.path.dirname(os.path.abspath(path)) or "."
    fd, tmp = tempfile.mkstemp(dir=d, prefix=".decisions-", suffix=".tmp")
    try:
        with os.fdopen(fd, "w", encoding="utf-8") as fh:
            fh.write(text)
            fh.flush()
            os.fsync(fh.fileno())
        with open(tmp, encoding="utf-8") as fh:
            if fh.read() != text:
                raise IOError("staged bytes differ from what was rendered")
    except BaseException:
        try:
            os.unlink(tmp)
        except OSError:
            pass
        raise
    return tmp


def cmd_check(argv):
    over, broken, strays, pending, nameless = [], [], [], [], []
    for project in PROJECTS:
        path = live_path(project)
        if not os.path.exists(path):
            continue
        _, entries, unknown, _carried = split(path)
        ordered = in_order(path, entries)
        pairs, bad, unresolved = superseded_pairs(ordered)
        bad = bad + ["  %s: Reverses #%s, which is in no live entry — check the archives, "
                     "and if it is nowhere the id is a typo" % (h[3:][:44], t)
                     for t, h in unresolved]
        if pairs:
            pending.append((project, pairs))
        bad = unreadable_reverses(ordered) + bad
        if bad:
            # SAID HERE BECAUSE `supersede` REFUSES ON IT. A reader who runs
            # check first should learn that the project cannot be superseded
            # before the command that cannot do it tells them.
            pending.append((project, None, bad))
        # An entry whose `Evidence` is not an engram id has no identity, so no
        # future ruling can reverse it — it can only be rewritten in place. That
        # is the honest cost of keying on the id, and it is reported rather than
        # papered over with a minted id that would point at no observation.
        no_id = [e for e in ordered if entry_id(e[2]) is None]
        if no_id:
            nameless.append((project, len(no_id), len(ordered)))
        with open(path, encoding="utf-8") as fh:
            n = sum(1 for _ in fh)
        arch = archive_files(project)
        flag = "  OVER" if n > WARN_LINES else ""
        found = [(path, unknown)] if unknown else []
        for a in arch:
            _p, _e, au, _ac = split(a)
            if au:
                found.append((a, au))
        if found:
            flag += "  %d UNRECOGNISED HEADING(S)" % sum(len(u) for _f, u in found)
            broken.append((project, found))
        print(
            "%-20s %5d lines  %3d entries%s%s"
            % (
                project,
                n,
                len(entries),
                "  (+%d archive file(s))" % len(arch) if arch else "",
                flag,
            )
        )
        if n > WARN_LINES:
            over.append(project)
        st = stray_temps(project)
        if st:
            strays.append((project, st))
            flag += "  %d STRAY TEMP(S)" % len(st)
    if strays:
        print()
        print("staged temporary files left in an archive directory —")
        print("a rotation cleans up after its own failures, so these are from a")
        print("process that was KILLED before it could. They are not decisions and")
        print("nothing reads them:")
        for project, found in strays:
            for f in found:
                print("    %s" % os.path.relpath(f, ROOT))
        print("check that the file beside them is intact, then delete them.")
    if pending:
        # NOT AN EXIT CODE. `hw status` prints one sentence per code and its
        # reader was already given the wrong remedy once by a code that meant
        # two things (see the split of 1 and 2 below). Supersession debt is not
        # a reason to stop anybody, so it is said and not signalled.
        print()
        print("rulings that a later ruling reverses and that are still in the live file —")
        print("they are read as standing decisions until they move:")
        for row in pending:
            if len(row) == 3:
                continue
            project, pairs = row
            for loser, winner in pairs:
                print("    %-18s %s" % (project, loser[1][3:][:56]))
                print("    %-18s   ^ reversed by %s" % ("", winner[1][3:][:52]))
        for row in pending:
            if len(row) == 2:
                print("    decisions supersede %s --apply" % row[0])
        for row in pending:
            if len(row) != 3:
                continue
            project, _none, bad = row
            print()
            print("%s cannot be superseded until these resolve:" % project)
            for line in bad:
                print("  %s" % line.strip())
    if nameless:
        print()
        print("live entries with no engram id in **Evidence:** — standing and readable,")
        print("but nothing can reverse them, because an id is the only name a ruling has:")
        for project, n, total in nameless:
            print("    %-20s %d of %d" % (project, n, total))
    if over: # MUTATION-ANCHOR: 100-M14
        print()
        print("over %d lines — rotate the oldest quarter out:" % WARN_LINES)
        for project in over:
            print("    decisions archive %s --apply    # keeps the newest %d, archives the rest" % (project, KEEP_DEFAULT))
    if broken:
        # Not cosmetic: until they are fixed `index` cannot see them. (`archive`
        # does NOT refuse over them — that was tried on 2026-09-08 and reverted
        # the same day because 77 of them blocked all four projects; it carries
        # them as body text of the entry above. This comment said "refuses" and
        # was left behind by that revert.)
        print()
        print("headings that are neither entries nor the archive pointer —")
        print("`index` cannot list them, and `archive` carries them as body text")
        print("of the entry above rather than as entries of their own:")
        for project, found in broken:
            for f, unknown in found:
                rel = os.path.relpath(f, ROOT)
                for n, heading in unknown:
                    print("    %s:%d  %s" % (rel, n, heading))
        print("give each one its date — `## YYYY-MM-DD[ HH:MM] <title>` — or demote")
        print("it to `### ` if it is a section inside an entry rather than one.")
    # TWO DIFFERENT ANSWERS, TWO DIFFERENT CODES. `hw status`'s only consumer
    # printed "a decisions.md is past the seek budget" for ANY nonzero exit, so
    # a file comfortably inside the budget with one undated heading was reported
    # as over it, on every hw status. A warning that is wrong is worse than none,
    # and one that is always on is one nobody reads. Judgment Day 2026-09-08.
    #   1 = over the seek budget (rotate it)
    #   2 = inside the budget, but headings this tool cannot read (fix them)
    # THREE ANSWERS, THREE CODES, for the same reason 1 and 2 were split on
    # 2026-09-08: `hw status` prints one sentence per code, so a code that means
    # two things makes it name the wrong remedy.
    #   1 = over the seek budget (rotate it)
    #   2 = headings this tool cannot read (fix them)
    #   3 = staged temporary files from a killed rotation (check, then delete)
    if over: # MUTATION-ANCHOR: 100-M14b
        return 1
    if broken:
        return 2
    return 3 if strays else 0


def cmd_index(argv):
    n = 25
    projects = []
    i = 0
    while i < len(argv):
        if argv[i] in ("-n", "--count"):
            if i + 1 >= len(argv):
                die("%s needs a number" % argv[i])
            n = int(argv[i + 1])
            i += 2
        elif argv[i] == "--all":
            n = 0
            i += 1
        else:
            projects.append(resolve(argv[i]))
            i += 1
    if not projects:
        projects = list(PROJECTS)
    for project in projects:
        paths = [live_path(project)] + archive_files(project)
        rows = []
        missed = []
        for path in paths:
            if not os.path.exists(path):
                continue
            _, entries, unknown, _carried = split(path)
            rel = os.path.relpath(path, ROOT)
            for lineno, heading in unknown:
                missed.append((rel, lineno, heading))
            # rank is the entry's chronological position within its own file, so
            # same-key rows come out newest-first instead of in append order,
            # and a live entry outranks an archived one on a tie.
            ordered = in_order(path, entries)
            live = 1 if not is_archive(path) else 0
            for rank, (key, heading, body) in enumerate(ordered):
                rows.append((key, live, rank, rel, heading, body))
        rows.sort(key=lambda r: r[:3], reverse=True)
        # WHAT A LATER RULING REVERSED, MARKED WHERE IT IS READ. Supersession
        # moves the loser out of the live file, so most reversed entries are in
        # an archive — and an archive is exactly where a reader has no way to
        # tell a standing ruling from a dead one. The pointer block promises
        # that archiving does not reverse anything, which is true and is not the
        # same as promising nothing in there was reversed. This is the
        # difference, said per row instead of left to be discovered.
        reversed_by = {}
        for _k, _l, _r, _rel, heading, body in rows:
            target = reverses_target(body)
            if target:
                reversed_by[target] = heading[3:]
        # Keyed on the ENGRAM ID, and across the live file and every archive at
        # once, because that is where the reader is: a reversal usually lives in
        # the live file while its loser has already been archived, so a lookup
        # confined to one file would mark nothing in exactly the common case.
        shown = rows if n == 0 else rows[:n]
        print("# %s — %d decision(s), newest first" % (project, len(rows)))
        for _key, _live, _rank, rel, heading, _body in shown:
            print("  %-28s %s" % (rel, heading[3:]))
            killer = reversed_by.get(entry_id(_body))
            if killer:
                print("  %-28s   ^ REVERSED by %s" % ("", killer))
        if len(shown) < len(rows):
            print("  … %d older — decisions index %s --all" % (len(rows) - len(shown), project))
        if missed:
            print("  ! %d heading(s) are not listed above because this tool does not"
                  % len(missed))
            print("  ! recognise them as entries. Not empty — UNREACHABLE by index:")
            for rel, lineno, heading in missed:
                print("  !     %s:%d  %s" % (rel, lineno, heading))
        print()
    return 0


def stage_everything(buckets, targets, project, path, preamble, keep,
                     carried, pointer_followers, staged):
    """Stage every archive file and the live file. Appends to `staged`.

    Extracted so a failure anywhere inside it has ONE cleanup boundary. The
    caller unlinks whatever landed in `staged` and refuses; that is the whole
    reason this is a function and not the loop it used to be.
    """
    for q in sorted(buckets, reverse=True):
        target = targets[q]
        existing_pre, existing = [], []
        if os.path.exists(target):
            existing_pre, existing, _u, _c = split(target)
            existing = in_order(target, existing)
        pre = existing_pre if existing_pre else archive_preamble(project, q)
        # SORT OLDEST-FIRST, THEN REVERSE. NOT `reverse=True`.
        #
        # This is the same defect this whole file was rewritten to fix, and it
        # was sitting inside the fix — found by Judgment Day 2026-09-08. Python's
        # sort is stable, so `reverse=True` keeps ties in INPUT order while
        # putting keys newest-first, and the input is oldest-first. Two entries
        # sharing a key (the common case: every undated entry collapses to
        # 00:00) therefore landed oldest-first inside a newest-first file, and
        # `in_order`, which reverses an archive file, read them back inverted:
        #     file order   ['A-older', 'B-newer']
        #     in_order     ['B-newer', 'A-older']   <- INVERTED
        # Sorting ascending and reversing the whole list moves the ties too.
        #
        # `existing` before `buckets[q]`: on a tie the already-archived entry
        # arrived first, so it is the older of the two.
        merged = list(reversed(sorted(existing + buckets[q], key=lambda e: e[0]))) # MUTATION-ANCHOR: 100-M13
        staged.append((target, stage(target, render(pre, merged))))
    # THE CARRIED TEXT GOES BACK AFTER THE REGENERATED BLOCK AND BEFORE THE
    # FIRST SURVIVING DATED ENTRY THAT ORIGINALLY FOLLOWED IT. Appended decisions
    # can sit below an older pointer; moving them ahead of this text changes
    # authored order while every count still passes. If none remain live, render
    # falls back to the existing end-of-file placement.
    trailer = list(pointer_block(project)) + list(carried)
    trailer_before = next((entry for entry in pointer_followers
                           if any(entry is live for live in keep)), None)
    staged.append((path, stage(path, render(preamble, keep, trailer, # MUTATION-ANCHOR: 100-M06
                                            trailer_before=trailer_before))))



def cmd_archive(argv):
    if not argv:
        die("usage: decisions archive <project> [--keep N | --before YYYY-MM-DD] [--apply]")
    project = resolve(argv[0])
    before = None
    keep_n = None
    apply_it = False
    i = 1
    while i < len(argv):
        if argv[i] == "--keep":
            if i + 1 >= len(argv):
                die("--keep needs a number")
            keep_n = int(argv[i + 1])
            if keep_n < 1:
                die("--keep must be at least 1 — an empty live file is not a rotation")
            i += 2
        elif argv[i] == "--before":
            if i + 1 >= len(argv):
                die("--before needs YYYY-MM-DD")
            m = re.fullmatch(r"(\d{4})-(\d{2})-(\d{2})", argv[i + 1])
            if not m:
                die("--before wants YYYY-MM-DD, got %r" % argv[i + 1])
            before = tuple(int(x) for x in m.groups())
            i += 2
        elif argv[i] == "--apply":
            apply_it = True
            i += 1
        else:
            die("unknown option: %s" % argv[i])

    path = live_path(project)
    if not os.path.exists(path):
        die("no %s" % path)

    # AN UNRECOGNISED HEADING IS CARRIED, AND THE CARRYING IS PROVED — it is not
    # refused, and it is certainly not dropped.
    #
    # Refusing was the first fix here and it was wrong, measured the same day.
    # There are 77 undated `## ` headings across the four projects' archive
    # files — section headings inside long entries, written as H2 — and every
    # project's merge target is 2026-Q3, so a rotation that refuses on them
    # refuses for ALL FOUR projects. `hw status` tells brainers to rotate an
    # over-budget file; a tool that cannot run stops guarding anything, and the
    # files keep growing.
    #
    # So an unrecognised heading stays what the parser makes it: body content of
    # the entry above it, or preamble. It travels with that entry into whichever
    # file the entry lands in, and the check below proves it arrived by
    # comparing entry bodies across the STAGED output before anything is
    # installed. `check` and `index` name these headings so they can be demoted
    # to `### ` or given a date — that is a formatting job, not this command's.
    inputs = [path] + archive_files(project)
    preamble, entries, _unknown, carried, pointer_followers = split(
        path, strict=True, with_pointer_anchor=True)
    if not entries:
        die("no dated entries in %s — nothing this tool can safely move" % path)

    # OLDEST FIRST, explicitly, by the file's own convention — see in_order.
    # Everything below slices this list from the END to keep the newest.
    ordered = in_order(path, entries)
    with open(path, encoding="utf-8") as fh:
        current_lines = sum(1 for _ in fh)

    # DEFAULT CUTOFF: the newest KEEP_DEFAULT entries stay, everything older
    # moves. By COUNT, deliberately — the calendar is not what makes these files
    # big. Measured 2026-08-24: 67 of one lane's 69 entries were in one quarter, so a
    # quarterly cutoff would have archived 2 entries off a file already at 2097
    # lines. `--before` is still there for a deliberate calendar split.
    refuse = None
    if before is not None:
        if keep_n is not None:
            die("pass --keep or --before, not both — they are two different cutoffs")
        keep = [e for e in ordered if e[0] >= before]
        move = [e for e in ordered if e[0] < before]
        # The same invariant --keep enforces, on the other cutoff. Without it a
        # --before later than every entry left keep empty and the plan line died
        # on keep[0] with a traceback instead of this file's own refusal.
        if not keep:
            die("--before %s is later than every entry, so it would leave the live "
                "file empty — an empty live file is not a rotation"
                % ("%04d-%02d-%02d" % before[:3]))
    elif keep_n is not None:
        keep = ordered[len(ordered) - keep_n:] if keep_n < len(ordered) else list(ordered) # MUTATION-ANCHOR: 100-M01
        move = ordered[:len(ordered) - keep_n] if keep_n < len(ordered) else [] # MUTATION-ANCHOR: 100-M01b
    else:
        # COUNT **AND** LINES, because the two disagreed. `check` flags a file on
        # lines; the rotation cut on entries alone, so one lane sat at
        # 1371 lines with 19 entries — under KEEP_DEFAULT, therefore "nothing to
        # archive" while hw went on nagging about it.
        #
        # The fix for that estimated each entry's cost and then, when the
        # estimate said everything fit on a file `check` called OVER, dropped a
        # hardcoded `len(entries) // 5`. A fraction is not a measurement, and the
        # estimate itself was the thing that was wrong. So: RENDER the
        # prospective live file and count its real lines, and take one more entry
        # out until it fits. No slack constant, no fraction, no estimate.
        if current_lines <= WARN_LINES and len(ordered) <= KEEP_DEFAULT:
            # Already inside both budgets. Stop here rather than adding a pointer
            # block that would push a file just under WARN_LINES back over it.
            keep, move = list(ordered), []
        else:
            n = min(KEEP_DEFAULT, len(ordered))
            while n >= 1:
                cut = len(ordered) - n
                keep, move = ordered[cut:], ordered[:cut]
                text = render(preamble, keep, pointer_block(project) if move else ())
                if line_count(text) <= WARN_LINES:
                    break
                n -= 1
            if n < 1:
                newest = ordered[-1:]
                text = render(preamble, newest, pointer_block(project))
                refuse = (
                    "%s: the newest entry alone renders %d lines against a %d-line budget "
                    "(%d of them preamble).\nrefusing: there is no retention this tool can "
                    "write that leaves the file inside the budget, and reporting a rotation "
                    "that does not is the bug this branch keeps reintroducing.\nsplit the "
                    "entry, or raise WARN_LINES deliberately."
                    % (os.path.relpath(path, ROOT), line_count(text), WARN_LINES, len(preamble))
                )
    if refuse:
        die(refuse)

    if not move:
        if current_lines > WARN_LINES: # MUTATION-ANCHOR: 100-M07
            # The old tool printed this same line and exited 0 on a file `check`
            # called OVER in the same breath. It is not a success.
            die("%s is %d lines, over the %d-line budget, and all %d entries are in "
                "the kept range — nothing this tool can move.\nthat is a refusal, not a "
                "rotation: the file is still over budget."
                % (os.path.relpath(path, ROOT), current_lines, WARN_LINES, len(keep)))
        print("nothing to archive: all %d entries are in the kept range" % len(keep))
        return 0

    buckets = {}
    for e in move:
        buckets.setdefault(quarter(e[0]), []).append(e)

    # Parse the quarter files this rotation merges into before the plan is
    # printed, so an unterminated fence in one of them is a refusal rather than
    # a plan for something --apply cannot do.
    targets = {q: os.path.join(archive_dir(project), "%s.md" % q) for q in buckets}
    for q in sorted(targets, reverse=True):
        if os.path.exists(targets[q]):
            split(targets[q], strict=True)

    print("%s: %d entries stay, %d move" % (os.path.relpath(path, ROOT), len(keep), len(move)))
    print("  keeping   %s .. %s" % (keep[0][1][3:][:48], keep[-1][1][3:][:48]))
    for q in sorted(buckets, reverse=True):
        print("  %s/decisions/%s.md  <- %d entry(ies)" % (project, q, len(buckets[q])))
    if not apply_it:
        print()
        print("this is the PLAN, not a result. re-run with --apply to write it.")
        return 0

    # WHAT IS EXPECTED AFTER THE WRITE, computed from the files as they are now.
    # The comparison is over entry CONTENT rather than a total, so it catches a
    # write that LOSES or DUPLICATES an entry, which a count cannot.
    #
    # AND IT DOES NOT CATCH THE 2026-09-08 INVERSION. An earlier version of this
    # comment claimed it did, and Judgment Day was right to call that a
    # contradiction: the bag is summed over the live file plus every archive, so
    # an inversion moves the same entries between those files and leaves the bag
    # unchanged — bag-in equals bag-out, exactly as 136 equalled 136. Order
    # correctness rests on `in_order` and on the tie handling in the merge, each
    # with its own driven arms in
    # setup/tests/100-decisions-rotation-order-and-refusal.sh. Two guards, two
    # properties; crediting one with the other's job is how the count came to be
    # trusted in the first place.
    before_bag = multiset(inputs)
    # The same inputs, line for line, for the conservation check in `audit`
    # that does not consult the region classifier. Blank lines are excluded:
    # `render` normalises separators, so they are formatting, not content.
    before_lines = {}
    for src_path in inputs:
        if not os.path.exists(src_path):
            continue
        with open(src_path, encoding="utf-8") as fh:
            for line in fh.read().splitlines():
                if line.strip():
                    before_lines[line] = before_lines.get(line, 0) + 1
    # The headings that were ALREADY unrecognised going in, keyed by TEXT and by
    # nothing else. Not by file: an unrecognised heading is body content of the
    # entry above it, so a rotation moves it between files on purpose, and keying
    # on the path made that legitimate travel look like corruption. Not by line
    # either — every line number in a rewritten file moves.
    #
    # The check below asks "did this write make something unreadable that was
    # readable before", not "does this project have formatting debt". `check` and
    # `index` own the second question.
    before_unknown = set()
    for src_path in inputs:
        if os.path.exists(src_path):
            _p, _e, u, _c = split(src_path)
            for _n, heading in u:
                before_unknown.add(heading)

    os.makedirs(archive_dir(project), exist_ok=True)
    staged = []

    def drop_staged():
        """Unlink every temp staged so far. Safe to call twice."""
        for _target, tmp in staged:
            try:
                os.unlink(tmp)
            except OSError:
                pass
        del staged[:]

    # A ROTATION THAT FAILS HALFWAY LEAVES NOTHING BEHIND. `stage()` cleans only
    # its OWN temp, so a multi-quarter rotation that failed on the second file
    # left the first one sitting beside the archives — and because it does not
    # end in `.md`, `archive_files()` ignores it and no command reported it.
    # Measured 2026-09-08 by injecting a failure into the second stage():
    #     setup/decisions/.decisions-u5r5llb_.tmp     and nothing named it
    #
    # The bare traceback goes too. This file refuses in its own words
    # everywhere else; a rotation that could not stage its files is not the one
    # place to print a Python stack at an operator.
    try:
        stage_everything(buckets, targets, project, path, preamble, keep,
                         carried, pointer_followers, staged)
    except SystemExit:
        drop_staged()
        raise
    except BaseException as exc:
        drop_staged()
        die("%s: could not stage the rotation (%s: %s). NOTHING was written and "
            "no temporary files were left behind."
            % (os.path.relpath(path, ROOT), type(exc).__name__, exc))
    def audit(paths, live):
        """-> (problems, live line count) for `paths`, a list of (file, label).

        The label is the DESTINATION's name even when the file is still a staged
        temp, so a problem reads the same before and after installation.
        """
        bag = multiset(f for f, _rel in paths)
        out = []
        for k, want in sorted(before_bag.items()):
            got = bag.get(k, 0)
            if got != want:
                out.append("  %s: was in %d place(s), would be in %d" % (k[1][:60], want, got))
        for k, got in sorted(bag.items()):
            if k not in before_bag:
                out.append("  %s: appeared out of nowhere (%d)" % (k[1][:60], got))
        for src_path, rel in paths:
            _pre, _ents, unknown, _c = split(src_path)
            for lineno, heading in unknown:
                if heading in before_unknown:
                    continue
                out.append("  %s:%d became unrecognisable: %s" % (rel, lineno, heading))
        # THE CARRIED TEXT IS CHECKED HERE, which is BEFORE the install. The
        # entry multiset cannot see it — it is not an entry — so a rotation
        # could drop it and still print "all N input entries accounted for
        # exactly once", which is exactly what happened until 2026-09-08. Two
        # different kinds of content need two checks; one of them was missing.
        with open(live, encoding="utf-8") as fh:
            live_lines = fh.read().splitlines()
        # LINE CONSERVATION, AND IT DOES NOT TRUST THE CLASSIFIER.
        #
        # The check below this one verifies that the CARRIED lines survive. That
        # is not enough, and finding out why is the most useful thing this
        # rotation learned: the subtraction runs BEFORE `carried` exists, so a
        # line wrongly classified as "the block we generate" is gone before the
        # check can look for it. A guard that consults the same classifier it is
        # guarding cannot see a classifier bug. Measured 2026-09-08: a fenced
        # example quoting this tool's own block was gutted and the carried-line
        # check passed it.
        #
        # So this compares the MULTISET OF NON-BLANK LINES across the live file
        # and every archive, before against after, and it knows nothing about
        # pointer regions. Two exemptions, both for text this tool writes on
        # purpose: the generated pointer block, which is rewritten every time,
        # and an archive preamble, which appears when a new quarter file is
        # created. Everything else must be conserved line for line.
        exempt = set()
        for line in pointer_block(project):
            if line.strip():
                exempt.add(line)
        for q in list(buckets) + [quarter(e[0]) for e in move]:
            for line in archive_preamble(project, q):
                if line.strip():
                    exempt.add(line)
        after_lines = {}
        for src_path, _rel in paths:
            with open(src_path, encoding="utf-8") as fh:
                for line in fh.read().splitlines():
                    if line.strip():
                        after_lines[line] = after_lines.get(line, 0) + 1
        # EXEMPT ONE OCCURRENCE, NOT THE TEXT. Exempting by membership was the
        # difference between a guard and a blind spot: a file that QUOTES this
        # tool's own block — a fenced example, say — has that line twice, and
        # exempting the text excused both copies. So exactly one occurrence is
        # forgiven on each side, which is the one this rotation rewrites, and
        # every further copy is ordinary content that has to be conserved.
        def outside_the_block(counts):
            return {ln: (c - 1 if ln in exempt else c)
                    for ln, c in counts.items()
                    if (c - 1 if ln in exempt else c) > 0}
        want_lines = outside_the_block(before_lines)
        got_lines = outside_the_block(after_lines)
        for line, n_before in sorted(want_lines.items()):
            n_after = got_lines.get(line, 0)
            if n_after < n_before:
                out.append("  a line would be LOST: on %d line(s) before, %d after: %s"
                           % (n_before, n_after, line.strip()[:60]))
        # COUNT WHAT WAS THERE, DO NOT ASSUME ONE. `n_before = 1` was wrong for
        # any line that legitimately repeats — a fence marker, a blank-ish
        # separator, a repeated bullet — and it reported "was on 1, would be on
        # 2" about a file that was perfectly correct. Measured 2026-09-08 on a
        # fenced example, whose ``` appears twice by construction.
        want_counts = {}
        for want in carried:
            if want.strip():
                want_counts[want] = want_counts.get(want, 0) + 1
        for want, n_before in sorted(want_counts.items()):
            n_after = live_lines.count(want)
            if n_after < n_before:
                out.append("  text below the archive pointer: was on %d line(s), "
                           "would be on %d: %s" % (n_before, n_after, want.strip()[:60]))
        return out, len(live_lines)

    # VERIFY THE STAGED BYTES, BEFORE ANYTHING IS INSTALLED. This is the whole
    # guard: an unrecognised heading is carried as body content rather than
    # refused, so what makes that safe is proving the content arrived — by
    # comparing the entry multiset, heading AND body, against the inputs. A
    # total was the signal that missed the real incident; it rotated 136 entries
    # into 136 entries and inverted them.
    #
    # The archive files this rotation does not open are part of the comparison
    # too: an entry must end up in exactly ONE place across the whole project.
    untouched = [a for a in archive_files(project) if a not in targets.values()] # MUTATION-ANCHOR: 100-M10
    staged_view = [(tmp, os.path.relpath(dest, ROOT)) for dest, tmp in staged] \
        + [(a, os.path.relpath(a, ROOT)) for a in untouched]
    live_tmp = staged[-1][1]

    # THE CUT WENT THE RIGHT WAY, ASSERTED. This is the 2026-09-08 incident's
    # own property and nothing above tests it: the multiset is summed over the
    # live file and every archive together, so a rotation that archived the
    # NEWEST entries and kept the oldest leaves it identical — 136 in, 136 out,
    # zero duplicates, file wrecked. What separates the two outcomes is that
    # everything moved must be OLDER than everything kept, and that is a
    # statement about order, so it takes an assertion about order.
    #
    # Ties are allowed through: two entries sharing a key are not ordered by
    # their own headings, so a cut between them is not this tool's to refuse.
    def cut_and_order(written):
        """-> complaints about the ORDER of what this rotation writes.

        Only the files it writes. An untouched archive with pre-existing
        disorder is somebody else's formatting debt, and refusing over it would
        block every rotation in the project — the shape that was tried with
        undated headings on this same day and reverted within hours.
        """
        out = []
        for src, rel in written:
            # `is_archive` reads the DIRECTORY, so it is right for a staged temp
            # too: `stage()` writes each temp beside its destination.
            _p, ents, _u, _c = split(src)
            out += order_problems(ents, is_archive(src), rel)
        if move and keep and max(e[0] for e in move) > min(e[0] for e in keep):
            out.append("  the cut inverted the file: the newest entry moved (%s) is "
                       "later than the oldest entry kept (%s)"
                       % (max(move, key=lambda e: e[0])[1][3:][:40],
                          min(keep, key=lambda e: e[0])[1][3:][:40]))
        return out

    problems, planned_lines = audit(staged_view, live_tmp)
    written_view = [(tmp, os.path.relpath(dest, ROOT)) for dest, tmp in staged]
    problems += cut_and_order(written_view)
    explicit = keep_n is not None or before is not None
    if not problems and planned_lines > WARN_LINES and not explicit:
        # Default mode chose the retention itself, so an over-budget result means
        # its own arithmetic was wrong. That is the bug, not a note.
        problems.append("  %s would be %d lines, over the %d-line budget"
                        % (os.path.relpath(path, ROOT), planned_lines, WARN_LINES))
    if problems: # MUTATION-ANCHOR: 100-M05
        for _dest, tmp in staged:
            if os.path.exists(tmp):
                os.unlink(tmp)
        die("refusing to install: the files this rotation would write do not carry "
            "what the inputs do.\n%s\nnothing was written; the live file and the "
            "archives are untouched." % "\n".join(problems))

    # ARCHIVES FIRST, LIVE LAST. There is no multi-file atomicity here: an
    # interruption between the two leaves entries in both places, which `index`
    # shows twice and the re-read below names. The other order loses them.
    installed = []
    try:
        for target, tmp in staged:
            os.replace(tmp, target)
            installed.append(target)
    except OSError as exc:
        for _t, tmp in staged:
            if os.path.exists(tmp):
                os.unlink(tmp)
        die("installing %s failed: %s\ninstalled so far: %s"
            % (target, exc, ", ".join(os.path.relpath(t, ROOT) for t in installed) or "none"))
    for target in installed:
        print("wrote %s" % os.path.relpath(target, ROOT))

    # AND RE-READ WHAT IS NOW ON DISK. The staged audit is about the bytes this
    # process rendered; this one is about the files anyone else will open, and it
    # is the only statement `archive` is entitled to print as a result.
    after_paths = [path] + archive_files(project)
    problems, final_lines = audit(
        [(a, os.path.relpath(a, ROOT)) for a in after_paths], path)
    problems += cut_and_order(
        [(t, os.path.relpath(t, ROOT)) for t in installed])
    _pre, final_entries, _u, _c = split(path)
    if problems:
        die("the installed files do not carry what the inputs did:\n%s\n"
            "the originals are recoverable from git; this tool wrote only the files "
            "named above." % "\n".join(problems))
    print("VERIFIED by re-reading: %s is %d lines / %d entries, %d entries across %d "
          "archive file(s), all %d input entries accounted for exactly once."
          % (os.path.relpath(path, ROOT), final_lines, len(final_entries),
             sum(len(split(a)[1]) for a in archive_files(project)),
             len(archive_files(project)), sum(before_bag.values())))
    if explicit and final_lines > WARN_LINES:
        # An explicit cutoff is an override, so it is honoured — but a file left
        # over budget by it is said out loud, never implied by silence.
        print("WARNING: --keep/--before is your cutoff and it leaves %s at %d lines, "
              "over the %d-line budget. `decisions check` will keep flagging it."
              % (os.path.relpath(path, ROOT), final_lines, WARN_LINES))
    return 0


def line_counts(paths):
    """-> {non-blank line: how many times it appears} across `paths`.

    Blank lines are excluded because `render` normalises the separators between
    entries: a blank is formatting this tool owns, not content anyone wrote.
    """
    counts = {}
    for src in paths:
        if not os.path.exists(src):
            continue
        with open(src, encoding="utf-8") as fh:
            for line in fh.read().splitlines():
                if line.strip():
                    counts[line] = counts.get(line, 0) + 1
    return counts


def conserved(before_bag, before_lines, after_paths, exempt_lines, live_after, carried):
    """-> complaints when `after_paths` does not carry what the inputs did.

    THE BEFORE STATE ARRIVES AS A SNAPSHOT, NEVER AS A PATH LIST, and that is
    not a style preference. This function is called twice — once on the staged
    temps and once on the installed files — and the second call happens after
    the inputs have been overwritten. Given paths it would re-read them and
    compare the result against itself, which passes no matter what was written.
    Measured while building this command: the second call reported the archived
    entry as having "appeared out of nowhere", because by then the live file it
    was re-reading no longer contained it.

    The same two comparisons `archive` makes, as a function both commands can
    call, and for the same reason: entries are compared by IDENTITY (key,
    heading and body, as a multiset over every file of the project at once) and
    everything else by LINE, so a class of content that is not an entry cannot
    fall through the gap between them. That gap is not hypothetical — text below
    the archive pointer went through it on 2026-09-08 under a printed
    conservation verdict.

    `exempt_lines` is the text this tool writes itself and rewrites on every
    run: the pointer block, and an archive preamble for a quarter file it
    creates. Exactly ONE occurrence of each is forgiven, never the text, so a
    file that quotes the block still has to conserve its copy.
    """
    out = []
    after_bag = multiset(after_paths)
    for k, want in sorted(before_bag.items()):
        got = after_bag.get(k, 0)
        if got != want:
            out.append("  %s: was in %d place(s), would be in %d" % (k[1][:60], want, got))
    for k, got in sorted(after_bag.items()):
        if k not in before_bag:
            out.append("  %s: appeared out of nowhere (%d)" % (k[1][:60], got))

    def outside_the_block(counts):
        # EXEMPT ONE OCCURRENCE, NOT THE TEXT. A file that quotes this tool's
        # own pointer block has that line twice; forgiving the text would excuse
        # both copies, and only one of them is ours to rewrite.
        return {ln: (c - 1 if ln in exempt_lines else c)
                for ln, c in counts.items()
                if (c - 1 if ln in exempt_lines else c) > 0}

    want_lines = outside_the_block(before_lines)
    got_lines = outside_the_block(line_counts(after_paths))
    for line, n_before in sorted(want_lines.items()):
        n_after = got_lines.get(line, 0)
        if n_after < n_before:
            out.append("  a line would be LOST: on %d line(s) before, %d after: %s"
                       % (n_before, n_after, line.strip()[:60]))
    with open(live_after, encoding="utf-8") as fh:
        live_lines = fh.read().splitlines()
    want_counts = {}
    for want in carried:
        if want.strip():
            want_counts[want] = want_counts.get(want, 0) + 1
    for want, n_before in sorted(want_counts.items()):
        if live_lines.count(want) < n_before:
            out.append("  text below the archive pointer: was on %d line(s), would be on "
                       "%d: %s" % (n_before, live_lines.count(want), want.strip()[:60]))
    return out


def superseded_pairs(entries):
    """-> ([(loser, winner)], [complaint]) for the live entries of one project.

    A ruling that carries `Reverses: <title>` replaces the ruling with that
    title, and the replaced one leaves the live file. That is the whole reason
    this exists: without it the live file only ever grows, because a decision
    that has been overturned is still a `## ` heading with a body, and `index`
    still lists it. Measured 2026-09-08 across the four projects, with the
    reversal declared in prose that no tool could read.

    IT RESOLVES BY ENGRAM ID, which is the whole reason `Evidence` is mandatory:
    the id is the entry's name, and `Reverses: #NNNNN` is another entry saying
    that name out loud. It used to resolve by title and that could not be made
    safe — see `NO_REVERSE`.

    IT RESOLVES WITHIN THE LIVE FILE ONLY. A `Reverses` naming something already
    in an archive has nothing to do: the entry is out of the live file, which is
    what the field is for. That is silence, not a complaint — otherwise running
    this command twice would refuse the second time, and an operation that
    cannot be repeated is one nobody can safely re-run after an interruption.

    What IS a complaint: a self-reference, an id carried by two live entries,
    and one entry reversed by two. All three are ambiguous rather than merely
    unusual, and this tool MOVES FILES — guessing here would archive the wrong
    entry and print a conservation verdict over the top of it, which is the
    exact failure this whole file is a reaction to.

    A duplicate id is worth its own case even though engram will not issue one
    twice: the id in the file is typed by a person, and two entries citing the
    same observation is a typo with no safe reading. Refusing names it; picking
    one buries it.
    """
    by_id = {}
    for e in entries:
        i = entry_id(e[2])
        if i:
            by_id.setdefault(i, []).append(e)
    pairs, problems, claimed, unresolved = [], [], {}, []
    for e in entries:
        target = reverses_target(e[2])
        if target is None:
            continue
        mine = entry_id(e[2])
        if mine is not None and target == mine:
            problems.append("  %s: Reverses names its own evidence (#%s)"
                            % (e[1][3:][:52], target))
            continue
        matches = by_id.get(target, [])
        if len(matches) > 1:
            problems.append("  %s: Reverses #%s, and %d live entries cite that id"
                            % (e[1][3:][:44], target, len(matches)))
            continue
        if not matches:
            # NOT IN THE LIVE FILE. Either it is already archived — which is the
            # whole point of the field, and must stay silent so the command can
            # be re-run after an interruption — or the id is a typo and names
            # nothing anywhere. Those two are indistinguishable here and only one
            # is benign, so the caller is handed the id and looks in the
            # archives. Until 2026-09-08 both simply `continue`d, and a mistyped
            # id produced "nothing to supersede" with exit 0 over a reversal the
            # author believed had happened — the exact no-op-wearing-a-success
            # this whole command exists to keep out.
            unresolved.append((target, e[1]))
            continue
        if target in claimed:
            problems.append("  #%s is reversed by two live rulings: %s and %s"
                            % (target, claimed[target][3:][:30], e[1][3:][:30]))
            continue
        claimed[target] = e[1]
        pairs.append((matches[0], e))
    return pairs, problems, unresolved


def cmd_supersede(argv):
    if not argv:
        die("usage: decisions supersede <project> [--apply]")
    project = resolve(argv[0])
    apply_it = False
    for a in argv[1:]:
        if a == "--apply":
            apply_it = True
        else:
            die("unknown option: %s" % a)

    path = live_path(project)
    if not os.path.exists(path):
        die("no %s" % path)
    inputs = [path] + archive_files(project)
    # THE SNAPSHOT, TAKEN BEFORE ANYTHING IS WRITTEN. Both verdicts below
    # compare against these two values, never against the files: the second
    # verdict runs after the inputs have been replaced.
    before_bag = multiset(inputs)
    before_lines = line_counts(inputs)
    preamble, entries, _unknown, carried, pointer_followers = split(
        path, strict=True, with_pointer_anchor=True)
    if not entries:
        die("no dated entries in %s — nothing this tool can safely move" % path)

    ordered = in_order(path, entries)
    pairs, problems, unresolved = superseded_pairs(ordered)
    # AN ID THAT IS IN NO FILE OF THIS PROJECT IS A TYPO, AND IT REFUSES. An id
    # sitting in an archive is the ordinary already-done case and stays silent,
    # so the command remains safe to re-run. Splitting the two is the whole
    # point: before this, both were silence, and a typo read as success.
    if unresolved:
        known = set()
        for src in [path] + archive_files(project):
            _p, ents, _u, _c = split(src)
            for e in ents:
                i = entry_id(e[2])
                if i:
                    known.add(i)
        nowhere = [(t, h) for t, h in unresolved if t not in known]
        if nowhere:
            problems += ["  %s: Reverses #%s, and no entry of this project — live "
                         "or archived — carries that id" % (h[3:][:44], t)
                         for t, h in nowhere]
    # A `Reverses` this tool cannot read is refused rather than ignored. Ignoring
    # it would leave a file whose author believes a reversal happened and whose
    # live entries say it did not, and that disagreement is invisible: the
    # command would print "nothing to supersede" and exit 0.
    problems = unreadable_reverses(ordered) + problems
    if problems:
        die("refusing: `Reverses` does not resolve to exactly one live ruling by "
            "engram id.\n%s\nnothing was written." % "\n".join(problems))
    losers = [loser for loser, _winner in pairs]
    loser_ids = {id(e) for e in losers}
    keep = [e for e in ordered if id(e) not in loser_ids]

    if not losers:
        print("nothing to supersede: no live ruling reverses another live ruling")
        return 0
    if not keep:
        die("every live entry is superseded — an empty live file is not a rotation")

    print("%s: %d ruling(s) stay, %d superseded move to the archive"
          % (os.path.relpath(path, ROOT), len(keep), len(losers)))
    for loser, winner in pairs:
        print("  %s" % loser[1][3:][:66])
        print("    reversed by  %s" % winner[1][3:][:64])

    buckets = {}
    for e in losers:
        buckets.setdefault(quarter(e[0]), []).append(e)
    targets = {q: os.path.join(archive_dir(project), "%s.md" % q) for q in buckets}
    for q in sorted(targets, reverse=True):
        if os.path.exists(targets[q]):
            split(targets[q], strict=True)
        print("  %s/decisions/%s.md  <- %d entry(ies)" % (project, q, len(buckets[q])))
    if not apply_it:
        print()
        print("this is the PLAN, not a result. re-run with --apply to write it.")
        return 0

    os.makedirs(archive_dir(project), exist_ok=True)
    staged = []

    def drop_staged():
        for _target, tmp in staged:
            try:
                os.unlink(tmp)
            except OSError:
                pass
        del staged[:]

    try:
        stage_everything(buckets, targets, project, path, preamble, keep,
                         carried, pointer_followers, staged)
    except SystemExit:
        drop_staged()
        raise
    except BaseException as exc:
        drop_staged()
        die("%s: could not stage the supersession (%s: %s). NOTHING was written and "
            "no temporary files were left behind."
            % (os.path.relpath(path, ROOT), type(exc).__name__, exc))

    exempt = set()
    for line in pointer_block(project):
        if line.strip():
            exempt.add(line)
    for q in buckets:
        for line in archive_preamble(project, q):
            if line.strip():
                exempt.add(line)

    untouched = [a for a in archive_files(project) if a not in targets.values()]
    staged_files = [tmp for _dest, tmp in staged] + untouched
    live_tmp = staged[-1][1]

    def verdict(after_paths, live_file):
        """-> every complaint about `after_paths`, identity AND sequence.

        THE SEQUENCE HALF IS NOT DECORATION, and it is the reason this function
        exists rather than a count. `conserved` sums its bag over the live file
        and every archive together, so an operation that moved the WRONG entries
        — the newest instead of the oldest, say — leaves that bag untouched and
        passes. That is not a hypothetical: it happened on 2026-09-08, 136
        entries in and 136 out, and the file was wrecked.
        """
        out = conserved(before_bag, before_lines, after_paths, exempt,
                        live_file, carried)
        for src in after_paths:
            _p, ents, _u, _c = split(src)
            out += order_problems(ents, is_archive(src),
                                  os.path.relpath(src, ROOT))
        # THE EXACT LIVE SEQUENCE, BY IDENTITY. Not "the same number of entries
        # stayed" and not "the survivors are in some chronological order" —
        # the survivors, named, in the order this command planned. An assertion
        # that a different set of entries could also satisfy is not an
        # assertion about THIS operation.
        _p, live_ents, _u, _c = split(live_file)
        want = [(e[0], e[1], canon(e[2])) for e in keep]
        got = [(e[0], e[1], canon(e[2])) for e in live_ents]
        if want != got:
            out.append("  the live file's sequence is not the one planned: "
                       "%d heading(s) expected, %d written, first difference at %s"
                       % (len(want), len(got),
                          next((w[1][3:][:48] for w, g in zip(want, got) if w != g),
                               "the end of the file")))
        # AND THE LOSERS ARE ACTUALLY OUT, by engram id — the same identity the
        # move was decided on, so this cannot pass by resolving differently from
        # the decision it is checking. The multiset above proves each entry is in
        # exactly one place; this proves that place is not the live file.
        live_ids = {entry_id(e[2]) for e in live_ents} - {None}
        for loser, _winner in pairs:
            if entry_id(loser[2]) in live_ids:
                out.append("  %s was superseded and is still in the live file"
                           % loser[1][3:][:60])
        return out

    problems = verdict(staged_files, live_tmp)
    if problems:
        drop_staged()
        die("refusing to install: the files this supersession would write do not carry "
            "what the inputs do.\n%s\nnothing was written; the live file and the "
            "archives are untouched." % "\n".join(problems))

    installed = []
    try:
        for target, tmp in staged:
            os.replace(tmp, target)
            installed.append(target)
    except OSError as exc:
        drop_staged()
        die("installing %s failed: %s\ninstalled so far: %s"
            % (target, exc, ", ".join(os.path.relpath(t, ROOT) for t in installed) or "none"))
    for target in installed:
        print("wrote %s" % os.path.relpath(target, ROOT))

    problems = verdict([path] + archive_files(project), path)
    if problems:
        die("the installed files do not carry what the inputs did:\n%s\n"
            "the originals are recoverable from git; this tool wrote only the files "
            "named above." % "\n".join(problems))
    with open(path, encoding="utf-8") as fh:
        final_lines = sum(1 for _ in fh)
    print("VERIFIED by re-reading: %s is %d lines / %d rulings, the %d superseded one(s) "
          "are in the archive, and every entry is in exactly one place, in order."
          % (os.path.relpath(path, ROOT), final_lines, len(keep), len(losers)))
    return 0


def main():
    argv = sys.argv[1:]
    if not argv or argv[0] in ("-h", "--help", "help"):
        print(__doc__.strip())
        return 0
    cmd, rest = argv[0], argv[1:]
    if cmd == "check":
        return cmd_check(rest)
    if cmd == "index":
        return cmd_index(rest)
    if cmd == "archive":
        return cmd_archive(rest)
    if cmd == "supersede":
        return cmd_supersede(rest)
    die("unknown command %r — check | index | archive | supersede" % cmd)


if __name__ == "__main__":
    raise SystemExit(main())
